/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Hypoxia is a shortage of oxygen reaching the body tissues.
   - PO2 is the partial pressure of oxygen in bar; FO2 is the oxygen fraction (0.21 in air); Pamb is ambient pressure in bar.
   - Cabin pressurisation keeps the cabin at a safe pressure altitude, often 6000 to 8000 ft.
   - Supplemental oxygen raises FO2 in the mask above 0.21, up to pure oxygen at high cabin altitude, so PO2 in the lungs stays near its sea-level value while Pamb is low.
   - Four supply types are common.
   - Crew masks use a regulator.
   - Other parts are tubing, a pressure gauge, a relief valve and an overboard discharge disc that bursts if the cylinder is overpressured.
   - Before flight the crew checks cylinder pressure and the mask seal.
   - Oxygen is not fuel, but it makes fuel and oil burn fiercely.
*/
